            {
               "text": "### The History of a Theorem\n\nGraph 1 in this section is a **visual proof** of the Pythagorean Theorem: instead of a long calculation, a picture shows why it is true (<htcite0>).\n\n#### Where do we find it?\n\nHave you ever asked yourself the following question?",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "fc17254b-5b9e-458b-8406-749d8fb6778a",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection: Pythagorean Theorem</b><br /><br />Why do I need to learn the Pythagorean Theorem?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Nowadays, many professionals use the Pythagorean Theorem every day, often without them even realizing it. In this section, we make that everyday use visible to answer the reflection question above. We will also clear up a common misconception about the history of this theorem.\n\n#### The discovery\n\nWhen this result was first discovered, it could not yet have been called the Pythagorean Theorem: it was already known long before Pythagoras was born (<htcite0>).\n\nWith that story in mind, we will follow Dr. Daniel F. Mansfield, who uses the term \"diagonal triple\" for a relationship between numbers governed by the same rule that the Greeks later attributed to Pythagoras. The Babylonians had already recorded this rule in ancient texts written in sexagesimal (base-60) positional notation (<htcite1>).\n\nOne reason we widen our view is that the values on the *Plimpton 322* tablet are similar to the ones we obtain with Pythagorean trigonometry. Although we cannot claim that the Babylonians used trigonometry the way the Greeks did—or the way we know it today—we can conclude that the piece *Si. 427*, kept at the Istanbul Archaeological Museum, proves that the Babylonians knew the right triangle (<htcite2>).\n\nBesides the Babylonians, records of the same relationship between the sides of a right triangle also appear in India (<htcite3>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "3d410035-deb7-4ecd-9da0-385faa97048e",
                     "text": "Shabnam Nasimi",
                     "page": "01:32 - 01:42 ",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b157e363-321b-4d0e-bf1c-72ac3a0a3f3f",
                     "text": "Mansfield, D. F.",
                     "page": "pp. 985 - 1003",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2021",
