            },
            {
               "text": "#### The Pythagorean Theorem\n\nThe version of the theorem we know today was written down in Ancient Greece (<htcite0>; <htcite1>), more than a millennium after the Babylonian inscriptions. The Greek philosopher and mathematician Pythagoras is said to have reached the same conclusion, either by learning of this knowledge from other cultures or through his own studies. This is the version we know in detail.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "082f655f-5086-4998-9ed6-02906ab5eeb0",
                     "text": "Moras, Roberto",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "f40bd766-4f57-4f9b-a715-dd75311cada5",
                     "text": "Britannica, T. Editors of Encyclopaedia",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "11",
                        "day": "03"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### The Multiplication Table and the Triangle\n\nAs we advance in our studies, some details go unnoticed. For example, when we draw the multiplication table as a chart, we can see that it looks like a right triangle.\n\nFor more details on the multiplication table and right triangles, we suggest studying Chapter 4 of the *First Steps* of **History Tracers** (<htcite0>). Triangles are also discussed in the text that teaches the multiplication table for 2 and 3 (<htcite1>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b5efc8f9-c268-430f-9681-7a43848ee48e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "First steps (Volume 1)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "72dcaa0f-07e3-41de-a637-c5b2f528dc33",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Understanding the Multiplication Table (2 and 3)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### The Right Triangle\n\nIn Graph 1, we have a right triangle (<htcite0>) built out of small squares. On each of its three sides we draw one large square, and every large square is a block of small numbered squares.\n\nOn the base, 16 small squares form a 4 × 4 square. We will call this side a **leg**. Opposite the angle theta (θ) (<htcite1>), 9 small squares form a 3 × 3 square, and one end of this side meets the corner of small square 4. We will also call this side a **leg**. On the remaining side, 25 small squares form a 5 × 5 square that joins the corner of small square 1 on the base to the corner of small square 1 on the other leg. We will call this side the **hypotenuse**.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "e31c5a5d-a61f-488b-a657-cf82d112e105",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "6438de6a-38b8-4060-943d-d2c3f5f36160",
                     "text": "Weisstein, Eric W.",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "",
                        "day": ""
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p>We see that the number of squares on each side (9, 16, and 25) is simply the length of that side multiplied by itself:</p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>9</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>3</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">x</mo><mn>3</mn></math><br /><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>16</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">x</mo><mn>4</mn></math><br /><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>25</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>5</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">x</mo><mn>5</mn></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 1</b>: Sides of the triangle expressed in squares.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection: This reminds me of...</b><br /><br />Look at <b>Calculation 1</b>.<br /><br />Can you write a mathematical relationship that connects the results of <b>Calculation 1</b>?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\" style=\"background-color: #FFFFFF;\"><p>When we add the squares of the two legs, we get the square of the hypotenuse:</p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mn>9</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><mn>16</mn><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><mn>25</mn></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Calculation 2</b>: Sum of the squares of the legs equals the square of the hypotenuse.</p><p>This is the Pythagorean Theorem. We write it as <b>Formula 1</b>:</p><p><math mathsize=\"1.5em\" displaystyle=\"false\"><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">+</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo lspace=\"0.1667em\" rspace=\"0.1667em\">=</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Formula 1</b>: The Pythagorean Theorem, where <i>a</i> and <i>b</i> are the legs and <i>c</i> is the hypotenuse.</p></div></div></center>",
               "source": [],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      }
